1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 14 - Playing Games

3
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:05,700
You like living in New York, don't you?

4
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,560
Oh, I love it.

5
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,040
It's so convenient.

6
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:09,720
I can take the bus to work...

7
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:11,620
or the subway or a taxi.

8
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,420
And there's so much to do.

9
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:16,400
Lots of movie houses, and the theater.

10
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:18,240
I know what you mean.

11
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,960
I'd like to live in the city,

12
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,080
but living in New Jersey and the suburbs

13
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:23,220
is better for Michelle.

14
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:24,600
Trees, grass.

15
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,880
There's a lot of good things about suburban living.

16
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:30,840
I grew up in Riverdale, remember?

17
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:32,040
So I know.

18
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,820
But, as a working woman,

19
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,380
I think New York has all the conveniences,

20
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:40,340
including the best tomatoes.

21
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:46,160
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

22
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,880
Michelle's the right age.

23
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:50,600
There are lots of things for her here.

24
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:52,220
You're right, Harry.

25
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,120
Today is the perfect example.

26
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:57,380
Michelle and her friends are at the aquarium in Brooklyn.

27
00:02:57,400 --> 00:02:58,900
They come back here for lunch,

28
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:01,800
then go uptown to the Museum of Natural History.

29
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,040
There's so much for young people to see and do.

30
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:05,580
It's just incredible!

31
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:06,920
Not just for young people.

32
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,960
What about me?

33
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:09,560
I've never been to the aquarium

34
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,660
or the Museum of Natural History. Have you?

35
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:12,880
Oh yes, Harry.

36
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:13,800
My mother and father

37
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,140
often took us somewhere in the city on the weekends.

38
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:17,780
Dad was a busy doctor,

39
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,280
but he usually managed to squeeze a Sunday in

40
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,560
with Richard, Robbie, and me.

41
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:24,780
I used to love to go to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

42
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,280
I've been there several times.

43
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,400
Twice with Michelle.

44
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,020
You ought to think about spending more time with Michelle

45
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,960
at all the great places in the city.

46
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:35,980
Well, perhaps,

47
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:38,820
you'll help me select some of the great places.

48
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,740
And perhaps, you'll join us?

49
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:45,720
Perhaps I will.

50
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:53,160
Well, there we are.

51
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:54,680
All set for lunch, Harry.

52
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:55,720
It looks inviting.

53
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,620
I wish Michelle and her friends would get here.

54
00:03:57,660 --> 00:03:59,120
I'm starving, aren't you?

55
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,920
I can't wait to take a bite of the pumpernickel.

56
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:03,680
It smells so delicious.

57
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,200
Coming! Coming!

58
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:10,580
Who is it?

59
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:11,980
Michelle.

60
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:13,460
It's us, Susan.

61
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:14,620
Come in, Michelle,

62
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:15,760
and bring your friends along.

63
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:16,920
We're on the fifth floor.

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,820
Oh, you've been here before.

65
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:24,060
Susan,

66
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:28,360
I really appreciate

67
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:29,760
your doing this for Michelle and her friends.

68
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:31,280
Oh, please, Harry.

69
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:31,920
It's nothing.

70
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:35,240
I'm not just doing it for Michelle.

71
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:37,260
I'm doing it for you.

72
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:39,700
Thank you.

73
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:45,040
I'm doing it for us, Harry.

74
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:48,060
Well,

75
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:51,560
it's... it's important for Michelle to see us together more often.

76
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:52,300
That's true.

77
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:54,240
It's important

78
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:56,400
for Michelle and me to get to know each other better.

79
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,400
That's important for us.

80
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,220
That makes me feel good.

81
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:06,320
What, Harry?

82
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:07,960
What makes you feel good?

83
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:09,980
Well, that you care about Michelle,

84
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:11,300
that you care about me, and

85
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:13,720
that you care about us.

86
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:17,780
Well, Harry, that's because I do.

87
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:20,120
I do care.

88
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:25,540
They'll be here any minute.

89
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:31,520
Susan, I'd like to continue this conversation later.

90
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:33,980
Of course, Harry.

91
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:37,360
We'll finish the conversation when they go to the museum.

92
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:39,440
I'd like that.

93
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:41,620
So would I.

94
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:55,780
I like living in the suburbs.

95
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:58,400
But living in New Jersey in the suburbs is better for Michelle.

96
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,060
Trees, grass.

97
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,720
We have a nice house with trees in front of it.

98
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,240
And a backyard for Michelle and her friends to play in.

99
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:07,140
It's a quiet neighborhood,

100
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:09,360
where people like to take long walks.

101
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,840
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

102
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:14,780
Michelle's the right age.

103
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,200
There are lots of things for her here.

104
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:19,360
Like today, for example.

105
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,200
Michelle and her friends went to the aquarium in Brooklyn.

106
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:24,800
Then they went to the Museum of Natural History.

107
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:26,920
I'd love to go there myself.

108
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:28,720
We could look at the dinosaurs.

109
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,200
And Michelle likes the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

110
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:32,720
We went there together.

111
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:34,520
And Susan likes the museum, too.

112
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:38,000
There's lots of things for us to do together in the city.

113
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:39,440
Like going to the South Street Seaport.

114
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:42,040
Oh, we had such a good time there.

115
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:43,360
And Central Park.

116
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,760
Oh, what a beautiful ride we took in the park.

117
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:48,140
All those trees and the sunshine.

118
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,440
I like the suburbs.

119
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,560
Or we could go to the Central Park Zoo.

120
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:54,640
Michelle loves animals.

121
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:57,160
You know, the city has so much to offer.

122
00:06:57,160 --> 00:06:58,840
So much for us to do together.

123
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,780
Maybe Michelle and I would like it here.

124
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:02,700
Hmm.

125
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:04,360
Maybe we would.

126
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:50,080
Oh, Daddy!

127
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:52,040
We had such a good time at the aquarium.

128
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:53,180
I saw a real shark.

129
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:54,560
I could almost touch it.

130
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,120
I'm so glad.

131
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:57,960
Hi. Michelle,

132
00:07:57,980 --> 00:07:59,620
why don't you introduce everybody to Susan?

133
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:00,420
Hi, Susan.

134
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:04,900
I'd like you to meet Audrey and her mother, Mrs. Cooper.

135
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:05,720
Hello, Audrey.

136
00:08:05,820 --> 00:08:07,100
Nice to meet you, Mrs. Cooper.

137
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:08,320
I'm Susan Stewart.

138
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:10,880
And this is Shirley and Nicole.

139
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:11,940
Hi, girls.

140
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:13,360
Come on in.

141
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:14,200
Come on in, Mrs. Cooper.

142
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:14,920
Come on in, everybody.

143
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:16,520
The aquarium was so exciting.

144
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:17,880
I had never been there before.

145
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:19,540
The girls learned a great deal.

146
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:20,880
Frankly, so did I.

147
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:21,940
Well, please sit down

148
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,060
and tell us all about it during lunch.

149
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:25,780
We prepared some tuna fish

150
00:08:25,780 --> 00:08:27,420
and cheese sandwiches for lunch.

151
00:08:29,100 --> 00:08:30,380
I love tuna fish.

152
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,400
Don't you, Audrey?

153
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:37,000
I remember when I used to be a Girl Scout.

154
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:39,080
My mother would take us everywhere, too.

155
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,320
Susan, I understand you're in the toy business.

156
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:43,400
Yes, I am.

157
00:08:43,540 --> 00:08:46,160
My company manufactures toys and games for children.

158
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:48,980
Susan's vice-president in charge of new toys and games.

159
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,980
And the marketing of new toys and games.

160
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,040
Can we test a new game for you, Susan?

161
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:54,960
That's an excellent idea, Michelle.

162
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:55,700
I happen to have

163
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:57,260
a game which I brought home to study.

164
00:08:57,340 --> 00:08:58,360
Let us try it!

165
00:08:58,500 --> 00:08:59,640
Do you want to play?

166
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:01,460
Nicole? Shirley? Audrey?

167
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:07,300
We tested it last week on twelve- to fifteen-year-olds,

168
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:10,080
and they found it to be too easy

169
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:11,880
in other words... boring.

170
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:15,200
We think it might be just right for ten- to twelve-year-olds.

171
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:16,460
That's us, Susan.

172
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:18,740
First, you shuffle the deck

173
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:22,620
and lay them face down.

174
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:25,600
Then you select the leader.

175
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:27,720
I'll be the leader.

176
00:09:28,740 --> 00:09:31,160
The leader takes the first ten cards

177
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:33,940
and lays them face up on this stand.

178
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:55,580
h-t-e-r-c-z-e-p-e-s.

179
00:09:57,260 --> 00:10:00,020
Everyone gets a turn, going counterclockwise,

180
00:10:00,040 --> 00:10:00,900
left to right.

181
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:07,640
You have thirty seconds to make a word,

182
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:09,880
using as many letters as possible.

183
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:12,600
You get one point for each letter,

184
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:17,740
plus the person with the longest word gets ten extra points.

185
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:20,840
The first one to get one hundred points wins.

186
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:21,940
That's easy.

187
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:24,220
OK, let's go around the table.

188
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:26,280
You first, Audrey.

189
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:41,060
Chest - c-h-e-s-t.

190
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:42,260
Good.

191
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:44,620
That's five points.

192
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:46,620
Next, Nicole.

193
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:59,020
Three - t-h-r-e-e.

194
00:10:59,460 --> 00:11:00,340
OK.

195
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:02,320
That's five points also.

196
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:03,980
It's your turn, Shirley.

197
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:16,160
Creep - c-r-e-e-p.

198
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:18,640
Creep.

199
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:21,040
That's another five-letter word.

200
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:21,820
Five points.

201
00:11:21,860 --> 00:11:23,280
So far, you're all tied.

202
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,740
Sheet - s-h-e-e-t.

203
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:27,460
Sheet.

204
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:30,780
Sheet is a five-letter word.

205
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:32,160
Michelle, your turn.

206
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:35,700
Cheese - c-h-e-e-s-e.

207
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:37,020
Terrific!

208
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:39,300
Michelle wins with a six-letter word,

209
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:42,400
plus she gets an additional ten points

210
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,120
for a total of sixteen points.

211
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:45,960
How are you enjoying the game so far?

212
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:48,120
I think it's too easy.

213
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:49,740
Too easy?

214
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:51,380
Michelle!

215
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:52,700
It's OK, Harry.

216
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:53,920
Michelle is quite right.

217
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:56,120
She just said it's too easy.

218
00:11:56,300 --> 00:11:57,920
Well, I like the game.

219
00:11:58,860 --> 00:12:00,440
I enjoyed lunch, Susan.

220
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:01,520
Thank you so much,

221
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:02,800
but we have to get going

222
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:05,480
to meet the rest of the troop at two o'clock at the museum.

223
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:07,580
Oh, it was nice meeting all of you.

224
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:09,900
I hope you have a wonderful time

225
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:11,740
at the Museum of Natural History.

226
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:12,640
Good-bye, everybody.

227
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:13,320
Have a good time.

228
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:13,860
Bye.

229
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:15,620
See you in front of the museum at five o'clock.

230
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:16,660
Oh, OK.

231
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:17,880
Thanks, Mr. Bennett.

232
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:18,920
That'll be fine.

233
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:21,800
And thanks again for the lunch and for the game.

234
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:23,840
Good-bye, Susan.

235
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:26,200
It's not a bad game.

236
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:29,200
It's just... slow.

237
00:12:29,420 --> 00:12:30,680
Just slow.

238
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:34,120
You helped save our company a lot of money.

239
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:34,860
Bye-bye.

240
00:12:44,660 --> 00:12:47,200
You are wonderful with kids.

241
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:50,020
I am.

242
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:54,660
Isn't there a conversation that we have to finish?

243
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:09,800
Hello, everyone. It's time to play Take My Word.

244
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,600
Here are the rules of the game.

245
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:14,740
We'll ask you to make some words from these letters.

246
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:18,200
We'll tell you how many letters are in the word, and we'll give you a clue.

247
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,180
Are you ready? Let's begin.

248
00:13:20,260 --> 00:13:24,480
If you're looking for a word with three letters, a three-letter word,

249
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:25,440
here's your clue.

250
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:29,280
You have five seconds. Go.

251
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:37,340
Time's up. Who says...

252
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:38,920
A cat, of course.

253
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:45,000
Now another word. It has four letters, a four-letter word.

254
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:45,960
Here's your clue.

255
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:48,040
Max is Susan's nephew.

256
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:49,720
So Susan is Max's...

257
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:50,760
Max's what?

258
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:53,020
You have six seconds. Go.

259
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:02,340
Your time is up. Susan is Max's...

260
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:03,300
Aunt.

261
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:09,100
Let's go on. Another four-letter word.

262
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:11,600
You can wear this when it's cold outside.

263
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:14,860
You have six seconds. Take my word.

264
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:23,960
All right. What do you wear when it's cold outside?

265
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:26,580
Well, I don't know about you, but I wear a coat.

266
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:30,120
Let's keep going.

267
00:14:30,140 --> 00:14:32,860
A three-letter word, and here's your clue.

268
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:34,840
You can do it with scissors.

269
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:37,520
Five seconds. Go.

270
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:47,820
Okay, ladies and gentlemen. You can use scissors to cut.

271
00:14:49,780 --> 00:14:53,540
And now, a word with five letters. Here's your clue.

272
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,080
Seven seconds. Go.

273
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:06,320
Time's up. The answer is...

274
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:09,280
Count. You can count with your fingers.

275
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:17,240
And now, friends, for the last word, you must use all ten letters, a ten-letter word.

276
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:18,440
And here's your clue.

277
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:21,780
What is Harry Bennett's occupation?

278
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:24,120
You have eight seconds. Go.

279
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:36,280
All right. Harry Bennett is an accountant.

280
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:43,320
And that's all the time we have. Thanks for playing Take My Word.

281
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:21,640
There's a conversation we have to finish.

282
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:24,300
What was it about?

283
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:26,300
It was about caring.

284
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:27,700
Oh?

285
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:31,220
Yes, caring.

286
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:34,240
I said that it makes me feel good

287
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:35,460
that you care about Michelle.

288
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:38,260
Well, I do care about Michelle.

289
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,720
And it makes me feel good that you care about me.

290
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:43,160
You know I do.

291
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:47,680
You're a... wonderful friend.

292
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:51,540
And it makes me feel good

293
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:53,940
that you care about us.

294
00:16:55,660 --> 00:16:56,800
Well...

295
00:16:58,060 --> 00:16:59,440
well, I do, Harry.

296
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:02,720
It's only natural that if I care about Michelle

297
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:04,820
and I care about you,

298
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:08,140
then I care about us.

299
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:09,120
It's OK, Susan.

300
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:10,940
We had this part of the conversation

301
00:17:10,980 --> 00:17:12,240
before Michelle and her friends arrived.

302
00:17:12,660 --> 00:17:13,620
Now comes the good part.

303
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:14,840
Like what?

304
00:17:15,740 --> 00:17:19,980
Well, assume that if you care about Michelle

305
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:21,160
and you care about me

306
00:17:21,180 --> 00:17:22,640
and you care about us,

307
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:25,380
that we can talk about us.

308
00:17:25,980 --> 00:17:27,300
I mean you and me.

309
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:30,580
Isn't that right?

310
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:35,100
What are you trying to say, Harry?

311
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:40,800
What I'm trying to say...

312
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:45,100
since this is so easy...

313
00:17:47,780 --> 00:18:10,920
Here are ten letters that express my feelings for you.

314
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:14,100
Don't be silly, Harry.

315
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:15,040
You want a hint?

316
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:15,980
It's two words.

317
00:18:16,780 --> 00:18:18,240
You're making the game harder.

318
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:21,640
Can't you figure it out?

319
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:24,280
Seven letters... two words...

320
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:27,080
that express the feelings that I feel for you in my heart.

321
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:29,280
Harry, I get it!

322
00:18:47,940 --> 00:18:49,740
Well? Does that mean you'll marry me?

323
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:54,960
Is that a proposal, or are we just playing a game?

324
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:02,300
Sit down, Susan.

325
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:07,820
There's something I have to ask you.

326
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:27,540
Susan, will you marry me?

327
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:30,160
It's such an important decision.

328
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:32,680
There's so many things to talk about.

329
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,100
There are three things that have to be done first, Harry.

330
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:38,300
Three things?

331
00:19:39,240 --> 00:19:41,920
First, we have to decide where to live.

332
00:19:42,580 --> 00:19:43,300
Well, that's easy.

333
00:19:43,380 --> 00:19:44,440
We talked about that earlier.

334
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:45,280
We'll live in New York.

335
00:19:45,700 --> 00:19:47,600
All right.

336
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:48,700
Second,

337
00:19:48,820 --> 00:19:52,580
we have to talk to Michelle together about being married.

338
00:19:52,620 --> 00:19:53,700
We'll do that immediately.

339
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:55,740
What's the third thing?

340
00:19:56,720 --> 00:19:58,760
You have to ask for my father's permission.

341
00:19:59,140 --> 00:19:59,980
Absolutely!

342
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:02,600
But first things first.

343
00:20:02,700 --> 00:20:03,940
We have to go pick up Michelle.

344
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:09,040
Oh, I'm so happy, Harry!

345
00:20:10,940 --> 00:20:12,380
Not as happy as I am.

346
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:37,640
It wasn't easy for me to do,

347
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:40,160
but I finally asked Susan to marry me.

348
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:43,200
Well? Does that mean you'll marry me?

349
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:47,400
Is that a proposal, or are we just playing a game?

350
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:51,680
I'm not just playing games.

351
00:20:51,880 --> 00:20:56,800
There's something on my mind I want to talk about.

352
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:59,040
It's hard for me to say,

353
00:20:59,080 --> 00:21:05,000
but I can spell it out for you to see easily.

354
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:09,360
I'm not just playing games.

355
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:10,520
I hope you know...

356
00:21:10,520 --> 00:21:14,460
I hope you know how much I really care for you.

357
00:21:14,660 --> 00:21:19,360
No matter where you are, I'm always there for you.

358
00:21:19,560 --> 00:21:23,400
I want to share my life with you.

359
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:26,880
So will you marry me?

360
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:29,880
I will always love you,

361
00:21:29,880 --> 00:21:34,280
and I'll try to make you happy every day.

362
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:37,080
Marry me.

363
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:40,800
I will always care for you,

364
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,480
we'll always have each other,

365
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:46,000
we'll be a family.

366
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:51,680
I'm not just playing games.

367
00:21:51,880 --> 00:21:54,560
If you could only read my mind,

368
00:21:54,560 --> 00:21:56,800
you'd know by now.

369
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,200
I'll spell it out for you,

370
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:03,820
so I can show you how I feel for you.

371
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:05,920
Two words.

372
00:22:06,760 --> 00:22:08,680
Marry me.

373
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:15,200
Marry me.

374
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:21,000
Marry me.

